[Term]
id: UBERON:0001211
name: Peyer's patch
namespace: uberon
def: "aggregations of lymphoid tissue that are usually found in the lowest portion of the small intestine ileum in humans; as such, they differentiate the ileum from the duodenum and jejunum // nodular lymphoid structures on the serosal surface of the small intestine." [Wikipedia:Peyer's_patch]
def: "the protruding lymphoid tissue located on the mucosa of the intestine that is composed of densely packed B cell follicles" [MP:0000696\,MESH\:A10.549.600\,PMID\:15841100\,UBERON\:cjm]
comment: AO notes: FMA has [set of?] [aggregated,small] lymphoid [follice,nodule] of [colon, SI, I, etc] - Pp is a syn for Aggregated lymphoid follicle of small intestine; but MA uses Pp as covering both SI and LI. MA also includes follicle as a part. WP says duodenum and jejunum lack PPs. Taxon notes: Reptiles have accumulations of lymphocytes in their guts that may be primitive Peyer's patches (Zapata and Solas).
subset: efo_slim
subset: pheno_slim
subset: uberon_slim
synonym: "aggregated lymphoid follicle of intestine" RELATED []
synonym: "aggregated lymphoid nodule" RELATED []
synonym: "noduli lymphoidei aggregati" EXACT LATIN []
synonym: "noduli lymphoidei aggregati intestini tenuis" EXACT LATIN [FMA:76466, FMA:TA]
synonym: "Peyers gland" RELATED [BTO:0001784]
synonym: "Peyers patch" RELATED [BTO:0001784]
