[Term]
id: UBERON:0001283
name: bile canaliculus
namespace: uberon
def: "An intercellular channel that takes up bile from hepatocytes, transporting it to the bile ducts." [MP:0008991]
def: "Fine tubular canals running between liver cells, throughout the parenchyma, usually occurring singly between each adjacent pair of cells, and forming a three-dimensional network of polyhedral meshes, with a single cell in each mesh[BTO]. A thin tube that collects bile secreted by hepatocytes. The bile canaliculi merge and form bile ductules, which eventually become common hepatic duct. Hepatocytes are polyhedral in shape, therefore having no set shape or design. They have surfaces facing the sinusoids, (called sinusoidal faces) and surfaces which contact other hepatocytes, (called lateral faces). Bile canaliculi are formed by grooves on some of the lateral faces of these hepatocytes. Microvilli are present in the canaliculi but are sparse. [WP,unvetted]." [BTO:0002841, Wikipedia:Bile_canaliculus]
comment: This class was created automatically from a combination of ontologies
