property_value: axiom_lost_from_external_ontology "relationship loss: part_of cardinal system (TAO:0000096)[TAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-08-14", external_class="TAO:0000186", ontology="TAO"}
property_value: external_definition "Bilaterally paired longitudinal vein; the anterior cardinal returns blood from the head, and the posterior cardinal returns it from the trunk; these two vessels join together on each side as the common cardinal vein (duct of Cuvier; misnamed the vitelline vein) that leads across the yolk cell to the heart's sinus venosus. Kimmel et al, 1995.[TAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-08-14", external_class="TAO:0000186", ontology="TAO", source="ZFIN:curator"}
property_value: external_definition "Bilaterally paired longitudinal vein; the anterior cardinal returns blood from the head, and the posterior cardinal returns it from the trunk; these two vessels join together on each side as the common cardinal vein, also known as the duct of Cuvier that leads to the heart's sinus venosus.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0011033", ontology="AAO", source="ZFA:0000186"}
property_value: homology_notes "In primitive vertebrates, the basic early embryonic pattern is retained, and blood from anterior and posterior systemic tissues is returned in anterior and posterior cardinal veins, both pairs of veins uniting in common cardinal veins near the heart. In derived vertebrates, the cardinals appear but usually persist only in the embryo, being functionally replaced by alternative adult vessels, the precava and postcava (anterior and posterior venae cavae).[well established][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0000112", ontology="VHOG", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/", source="ISBN:978-0072528305 Kardong KV, Vertebrates: Comparative Anatomy, Function, Evolution (2006) p.462"}
property_value: taxon_notes "Paired in gnathostomes; with only a vestige of the right duct in the adult hagfish (21) and the left duct atrophying at metamorphosis in the lamprey (22)[PMID:20959416, S1]" xsd:string

[Term]
id: UBERON:0002065
name: posterior cardinal vein
namespace: uberon
def: "One of two paired cardinal veins that return blood from the body of the embryo[Kardong]" [ISBN10:0073040584, Wikipedia:Posterior_cardinal_vein]
def: "One of two paired cardinal veins that return blood from the body of the embryo[Kardong]" [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posterior_cardinal_vein, ISBN10:0073040584]
comment: ...join with the corresponding right and left cardinal veins to form the left common cardinal veins, which empty in the sinus venosus. Most of the posterior cardinal veins regress, what remains of them forms the renal segment of the inferior vena cava and the common iliac veins. Later in the development stages, the posterior cardinal veins are replaced by the subcardinal and supracardinal veins. The subcardinal veins form part of the inferior vena cava, renal veins and gonadal veins. The supracardinal veins form part of the inferior vena cava, the intercostal veins, hemiazygos vein and azygos vein[WP]
subset: pheno_slim
subset: uberon_slim
subset: vertebrate_core
