[Term]
id: UBERON:0004868
name: tapetum lucidum
name: tapetum lucidum of camera-type eye
namespace: uberon
def: "A layer of tissue in the eye of many vertebrate animals, that lies immediately behind or sometimes within the retina. It reflects visible light back through the retina, increasing the light available to the photoreceptors" [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapetum_lucidum, http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/14738502]
def: "a layer of tissue in the eye of many vertebrate animals, that lies immediately behind or sometimes within the retina. It reflects visible light back through the retina, increasing the light available to the photoreceptors" [PMID:14738502, Wikipedia:Tapetum_lucidum]
comment: Function notes: It normally functions at low light levels to provide the light-sensitive retinal cells with a second opportunity for photon-photoreceptor stimulation, thereby enhancing visual sensitivity
synonym: "tapeta lucida" EXACT PLURAL []
synonym: "tapetum lucidum" EXACT []
synonym: "tapis luisant@fr" EXACT []
xref: http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/TapetumLucidum.JPG
xref: Tapetum:lucidum
xref: Wikipedia:Tapetum_lucidum
is_a: UBERON:0000479 ! tissue
is_a: UBERON:0004121 ! ectoderm-derived structure
is_a: UBERON:0015169 ! tapetum
relationship: foaf-depicted_by http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/76/TapetumLucidum.JPG
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:8782 ! Aves
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:9321
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:9443 ! Primates
relationship: never_in_taxon NCBITaxon:9822
relationship: part_of UBERON:0010230 ! eyeball of camera-type eye
property_value: external_definition "The tapetum lucidum is a biologic reflector system that is a common feature in the eyes of vertebrates. Typically absent in diurnal animals. In vertebrates, the tapetum lucidum exhibits diverse structure, organization and composition. The tapetum lucidum represents a remarkable example of neural cell and tissue specialization as an adaptation to a dim light environment and, despite these differences, all tapetal variants act to increase retinal sensitivity by reflecting light back through the photoreceptor layer. These variations regarding both its location and structure, as well as the choice of reflective material, may represent selective visual adaptations associated with their feeding behavior, in response to the use of specific wavelengths and amount of reflectance required.[PMID:14738502]." xsd:string {source="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/14738502"}
property_value: function_notes "It normally functions at low light levels to provide the light-sensitive retinal cells with a second opportunity for photon-photoreceptor stimulation, thereby enhancing visual sensitivity" xsd:string
property_value: has_relational_adjective "tapetal" xsd:string
property_value: taxon_notes "Two morphologic types of tapetum lucidum can be observed in the eyes of fish: one in the choroid that occurs in elasmobranchs, and the other which occurs in teleosts and lies in the pigment epithelium of the retina" xsd:string {source="http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/14738502"}

[Term]
id: UBERON:0004869
name: parietal organ
namespace: uberon
def: "A part of the epithalamus present in some animal species. The eye may be photoreceptive and is usually associated with the pineal gland, regulating circadian rhythmicity and hormone production for thermoregulation. The parietal eye is a part of the epithalamus, which can be divided into two major parts; the epiphysis (the pineal organ, or pineal gland if mostly endocrine) and the parietal organ (often called the parietal eye, or third eye if it is photoreceptive). It arises as an anterior evagination of the pineal organ or as a separate outgrowth of the roof of the diencephalon. In some species, it protrudes through the skull.[4] The parietal eye uses a different biochemical method of detecting light than rod cells or cone cells in a normal vertebrate eye[WP]." [Wikipedia:Parietal_eye]
def: "A part of the epithalamus present in some animal species. The eye may be photoreceptive and is usually associated with the pineal gland, regulating circadian rhythmicity and hormone production for thermoregulation. The parietal eye is a part of the epithalamus, which can be divided into two major parts; the epiphysis (the pineal organ, or pineal gland if mostly endocrine) and the parietal organ (often called the parietal eye, or third eye if it is photoreceptive). It arises as an anterior evagination of the pineal organ or as a separate outgrowth of the roof of the diencephalon. In some species, it protrudes through the skull.[4] The parietal eye uses a different biochemical method of detecting light than rod cells or cone cells in a normal vertebrate eye[WP]." [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parietal_eye]
synonym: "parietal eye" EXACT [Wikipedia:Parietal_eye]
synonym: "parietal eye" EXACT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parietal_eye]
synonym: "third eye" RELATED [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parietal_eye]
synonym: "third eye" RELATED [Wikipedia:Parietal_eye]
xref: Parietal:eye
xref: Wikipedia:Parietal_eye
