relationship: foaf-depicted_by http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Gray865.png
relationship: homologous_in NCBITaxon:7742 ! Vertebrata
relationship: located_in UBERON:0003098 ! optic stalk
relationship: part_of UBERON:0003072 ! optic cup
relationship: part_of UBERON:0016855 ! ventral part of optic cup
property_value: external_definition "A break in the continuity of the optic cup where the two sides of the cup meet.[AAO]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-06-20", external_class="AAO:0011026", ontology="AAO", source="ISBN:0815318960"}
property_value: homology_notes "The folded arrangement of the vertebrate retina and RPE [retinal pigment epithelial] provides an evolutionary explanation for the occurrence of the choroid fissure, as proposed more than a century ago. Early in evolution, before the optic cup invaginated, the axons from retinal ganglion cells would simply have run over the surface of the structure. Hence, one can view the optic nerve as having acted rather like a rope in linking the retina to higher centres: the developing eye cup has simply wrapped around this 'rope', and the developing axons have thereby not needed to penetrate the retina.[well established][VHOG]" xsd:string {date_retrieved="2012-09-17", external_class="VHOG:0000655", ontology="VHOG", source="http://bgee.unil.ch/", source="DOI:10.1038/nrn2283 Lamb TD, Collin SP and Pugh EN, Evolution of the vertebrate eye: opsins, photoreceptors, retina and eye cup. Nature Reviews Neuroscience (2007)"}

[Term]
id: UBERON:0005413
name: spinocerebellar tract
namespace: uberon
def: "The spinocerebellar tract is a set of axonal fibers originating in the spinal cord and terminating in the ipsilateral cerebellum. This tract conveys information to the cerebellum about limb and joint position." [Wikipedia:Spinocerebellar_tract]
def: "This tract conveys interoceptive, proprioceptive, and exteroceptive information from the body's internal organs, and from the trunk, extremities, and neck region to the Cerebellum." [NIFSTD:nlx_anat_1005036]
