[Term]
id: UBERON:0007805
name: synsacrum
namespace: uberon
def: "A region of the lower vertebral column formed from the fusion of vertebrae." [UBERON:cjm]
def: "The synsacrum is a skeletal structure, mainly described in birds and dinosaurs, in which the sacrum is extended by incorporation of additional fused or partially-fused caudal or lumbar vertebrae. The ilium of the pelvis is attached to the synsacrum and, in some species, may also be fused to it. Together with the pelvis this forms a rigid structure which carries the weight of the hind end of the animal. In living and some prehistoric birds, posterior to the synsacrum there are usually only a few free caudal vertebrae, on the end of which lies the pygostyle[WP]. Complex of synostotically fused vertebrae supporting the pelvic girdle. In anurans, the synsacrum is formed by two vertebrae and the urostyle is fused to its posterior end[AAO]." [AAO:Pugener_2002, Wikipedia:Synsacrum]
comment: produced by fusion of last thoracics, all lumbars, all sacrals, & first few caudals;
xref: AAO:0000740
xref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synsacrum
xref: http://people.eku.edu/ritchisong/birdskeleton.gif
xref: http://www.snomedbrowser.com/Codes/Details/67943001
xref: SCTID:67943001
xref: Wikipedia:Synsacrum
