[Term]
id: UBERONTEMP:41980771-8a82-40d2-877e-3156cc80857b
name: metotic fissure
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/41980771-8a82-40d2-877e-3156cc80857b
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/8a9f15fd-f71d-4ce3-8c65-6dc4f5fc93b5
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/b43afb8f-4622-4a65-9a1c-94f8f518658f
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/da5e761a-d170-4b90-8981-8938be2f4c0c
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/df6059e8-0f1f-4c0b-b773-c80670b81faa
alt_id: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/provisional/fcd3d1c4-4eb6-4aee-b0da-479cac0aed4f
def: "In the embryos of Recent gnathostomes, the vagal nerve exits the developing braincase through the metotic fissue is a fundamental division of the chondrocranium that separates the otic capsule from the occipital region of the skull or vertebral column (Goodrich 1930, p. 232). The meiotic fissure can be recognized as a persistent feature in some actinopterygians, as well as Acanthodes, some sarcopterygians and chondrichthians (Coates and Sequeira 1998).  In the adult a persistent meiotic fissure is known as the otico-occipital fissure. From Major Events in Early Vertebrate Evolution, edited by Per Erik Ahlberg." []
synonym: "otico-occipital fissure" EXACT []
is_a: UBERON:0006846 ! surface groove
relationship: part_of UBERON:0002241 ! chondrocranium
